.\" OpenBSD-style concise man page
.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt DD 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dd
.Nd convert and copy a file
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm dd
.Op Ar operand ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility copies the standard input to the standard output,
applying any specified conversions.
Input data is read and written in 512-byte blocks.
Input and output block sizes, conversion rules,
and skip/seek offsets may be specified using operands.
.Pp
The following operands are available:
.Bl -tag -width "status=LEVEL"
.It Cm if Ns = Ns Ar file
Read from
.Ar file
instead of the standard input.
.It Cm of Ns = Ns Ar file
Write to
.Ar file
instead of the standard output.
By default the output file is truncated;
use
.Cm conv=notrunc
to preserve existing data beyond the copied region.
.It Cm bs Ns = Ns Ar n
Set both input and output block size to
.Ar n
bytes, superseding
.Cm ibs
and
.Cm obs .
When
.Cm bs
is specified, each input block is copied to the output
as a single block without aggregating short reads.
.It Cm ibs Ns = Ns Ar n
Set the input block size to
.Ar n
bytes (default 512).
.It Cm obs Ns = Ns Ar n
Set the output block size to
.Ar n
bytes (default 512).
.It Cm count Ns = Ns Ar n
Copy only
.Ar n
input blocks.
.It Cm skip Ns = Ns Ar n
Skip
.Ar n
input blocks before copying.
.It Cm seek Ns = Ns Ar n
Seek past
.Ar n
output blocks before copying.
.It Cm conv Ns = Ns Ar value Ns Op , Ns Ar value ...
Apply one or more conversions, separated by commas:
.Bl -tag -width "noerror" -compact
.It Cm lcase
Map uppercase characters to lowercase.
.It Cm ucase
Map lowercase characters to uppercase.
.It Cm notrunc
Do not truncate the output file.
.It Cm noerror
Continue processing after read errors.
Unlike GNU
.Nm ,
which retries a failing region without bound, each failed read
consumes one block of the
.Cm count
budget, so
.Cm count Ns = Ns Ar n
bounds the number of attempts.
On a seekable input the bad block is skipped by seeking forward one
.Cm ibs .
Both failure modes are bounded so that, even with no
.Cm count ,
.Nm
always terminates rather than spinning forever.
An input that can be neither read nor seeked past (such as a pipe)
aborts after a small bounded number of consecutive zero-progress
attempts.
An input that seeks past every bad block yet never reads a single
block successfully aborts after a much larger bounded number of
consecutive skipped blocks.
Where GNU
.Nm
would retry such a region without bound, bound it explicitly with
.Cm count Ns = Ns Ar n .
.It Cm sync
Pad each input block with NUL bytes to
.Cm ibs
size.
.El
.It Cm status Ns = Ns Ar level
Control the information printed to standard error:
.Bl -tag -width "progress" -compact
.It Cm none
Suppress all informational messages.
.It Cm noxfer
Suppress the transfer statistics line.
.It Cm progress
Print transfer statistics (default behavior).
.El
.El
.Pp
Where sizes are expected, the following suffixes may be used:
.Cm c Ns =1,
.Cm w Ns =2,
.Cm b Ns =512,
.Cm k Ns =1024,
.Cm K Ns =1024,
.Cm M Ns =1048576,
.Cm G Ns =1073741824.
Sizes may also use
.Ql x
for multiplication (e.g.,
.Dq 1024x1024 ) .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std dd
.Sh EXAMPLES
Copy a file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ dd if=input.bin of=output.bin
.Ed
.Pp
Create a 1\ MB file filled with zeros:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ dd if=/dev/zero of=file.img bs=1024 count=1024
.Ed
.Pp
Convert a file to uppercase:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ dd if=lower.txt of=upper.txt conv=ucase
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cp 1 ,
.Xr tr 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is compliant with the
.St -p1003.1-2008
specification.
